      "content": "You are Step 3 (Reviewer) in a 3-step pipeline: Planner → Executor → [YOU].\n\nAudit whether the code implements the key decisions and is correct, secure, and efficient.\n\nRULES:\n1. Be HONEST. Score 95+ = near-perfect (rare). Most real code scores 70-90.\n2. Every issue MUST cite exact code — quote the function/line and explain the fix. No vague claims.\n3. Before claiming something is MISSING, search the code for it. False claims invalidate your review.\n4. Check every key decision — missed decision = compliance deduction.\n5. Truncated code or placeholders = syntax score 0, compliance halved.\n6. Respond with ONLY the JSON object. No markdown fences, no preamble.\n\nSCORING: 90-100 production-ready | 75-89 minor issues | 50-74 significant bugs | 25-49 broken | 0-24 no usable code\n\n═══ REQUIREMENT ═══\nBuild a rate limiter middleware for Express that supports sliding window, per-user limits, and returns proper 429 responses with Retry-After headers\n\n═══ KEY DECISIONS (check each one) ═══\n  1. Redis Sorted Sets (ZSets) over simple fixed-window counters because ZSets allow precise sliding window behavior (counting requests within any moving time window) rather than resetting at fixed intervals, preventing request spikes at boundary times.\n  2. Lua scripting over client-side check-and-set logic because Lua scripts in Redis are atomic, preventing race conditions where multiple concurrent requests check the limit simultaneously and both pass.\n  3. Factory function pattern over singleton instance because different routes may require different rate limit configurations (e.g., strict API vs. lenient web endpoints) without creating global state conflicts.\n  4. Fail-open strategy over fail-closed on Redis connectivity loss because denying all service due to a middleware dependency failure is riskier for system availability than temporarily allowing extra load.\n  5. Milliseconds precision over seconds for internal tracking because high-traffic systems require granular window management to prevent burst spikes at second boundaries.\n  6. Dynamic Retry-After calculation based on oldest request timestamp over generic fixed delay because it accurately informs the client when the sliding window will actually open for their specific request history.\n\n═══ CODE ═══\nFiles: src/types.ts, src/lua/slidingWindow.lua, src/RateLimiter.ts, src/middleware.ts\n\n// ═══ src/types.ts ═══\nimport { Redis } from 'ioredis';\nimport { Request } from 'express';\n\nexport interface RateLimitOptions {\n  windowMs: number;\n  maxRequests: number;\n  redisClient: Redis;\n  keyGenerator?: (req: Request) => string;\n  skipFailedRequests?: boolean;\n}\n\nexport interface RateLimitResult {\n  allowed: boolean;\n  currentCount: number;\n  retryAfter?: number;\n}\n\n// ═══ src/lua/slidingWindow.lua ═══\nconst slidingWindowScript = `\nlocal key = KEYS[1]\nlocal now = tonumber(ARGV[1])\nlocal windowMs = tonumber(ARGV[2])\nlocal limit = tonumber(ARGV[3])\n\n-- Remove expired entries (older than now - windowMs)\nredis.call('ZREMRANGEBYSCORE', key, '-inf', now - windowMs)\n\n-- Add current timestamp to the sorted set\nredis.call('ZADD', key, now, now)\n\n-- Set expiration for the key to clean up automatically\nredis.call('EXPIRE', key, math.floor(windowMs / 1000) + 1)\n\n-- Get the count of requests in the window\nlocal count = redis.call('ZCARD', key)\n\n-- If over the limit, get the oldest timestamp to calculate retryAfter\nif count > limit then\n  local oldest = redis.call('ZRANGEBYSCORE', key, '-inf', '+inf', 'LIMIT', 0, 1)[1]\n  return {count, tonumber(oldest)}\nend\n\nreturn {count, nil}\n`;\n\nexport default slidingWindowScript;\n\n// ═══ src/RateLimiter.ts ═══\nimport { Redis } from 'ioredis';\nimport { RateLimitResult } from './types';\nimport slidingWindowScript from './lua/slidingWindow';\n\nclass RateLimiter {\n  constructor(\n    private redis: Redis,\n    private windowMs: number,\n    private limit: number\n  ) {}\n\n  async limit(key: string): Promise<RateLimitResult> {\n    const now = Date.now();\n    \n    try {\n      const results = await this.redis.eval(\n        slidingWindowScript,\n        1, // Number of keys\n        key,\n        now.toString(),\n        this.windowMs.toString(),\n        this.limit.toString()\n      ) as [number, number | null];\n      \n      const count = results[0];\n      const allowed = count <= this.limit;\n      \n      let retryAfter: number | undefined;\n      if (!allowed) {\n        const oldest = results[1];\n        if (oldest !== null) {\n          retryAfter = Math.ceil((oldest + this.windowMs - now) / 1000);\n        }\n      }\n      \n      return { allowed, currentCount: count, retryAfter };\n    } catch (error) {\n      // Fail-open strategy: if Redis is unavailable, allow the request\n      console.error('Rate limiter error:', error);\n      return { allowed: true, currentCount: 0 };\n    }\n  }\n}\n\nexport default RateLimiter;\n\n// ═══ src/middleware.ts ═══\nimport { Request, Response, NextFunction } from 'express';\nimport { RateLimitOptions } from './types';\nimport RateLimiter from './RateLimiter';\n\nfunction rateLimit(options: RateLimitOptions): (req: Request, res: Response, next: NextFunction) => Promise<void> {\n  const limiter = new RateLimiter(\n    options.redisClient,\n    options.windowMs,\n    options.maxRequests\n  );\n  \n  return async (req: Request, res: Response, next: NextFunction) => {\n    // Default key generator uses IP address only, allowing users to access different endpoints independently\n    const key = options.keyGenerator ? options.keyGenerator(req) : req.ip || 'unknown';\n    \n    try {\n      const result = await limiter.limit(key);\n      \n      // Set standard rate limiting headers\n      res.setHeader('X-RateLimit-Limit', options.maxRequests);\n      res.setHeader('X-RateLimit-Remaining', Math.max(0, options.maxRequests - result.currentCount));\n      \n      if (!result.allowed) {\n        // Include Retry-After header with dynamically calculated value\n        if (result.retryAfter) {\n          res.setHeader('Retry-After', result.retryAfter);\n        }\n        \n        return res.status(429).json({\n          error: 'Too Many Requests',\n          retryAfter: result.retryAfter\n        });\n      }\n      \n      next();\n    } catch (err) {\n      console.error('Unexpected error in rate limiter middleware:', err);\n      // Fail-open: continue processing if there's an unexpected error\n      next();\n    }\n  };\n}\n\nexport default rateLimit;\n\n═══ COMPILATION ═══\nSyntax Validated\n\nRUBRIC (100 pts): Compliance (40) — missed decision -5, missing step -10. Security (20). Efficiency (20). Syntax (20).\n\nOutput JSON:\n{\n  \"score\": <0-100>,\n  \"breakdown\": { \"compliance\": <0-40>, \"security\": <0-20>, \"efficiency\": <0-20>, \"syntax\": <0-20> },\n  \"issues\": [\"Actionable: 'Function X has bug Y — fix by doing Z'\"],\n  \"decisionsHonored\": [\"Each key decision and whether it was implemented\"],\n  \"summary\": \"One-paragraph verdict — lead with most critical finding\",\n  \"compilationStatus\": \"Syntax Validated\",\n  \"crystallizable_insight\": \"If score > 90: reusable pattern. Otherwise null.\"\n}"
