ck(key, config): Promise<RateLimitResult>. Algorithm: try primaryStorage.checkAndIncrement; on error, log warning, if fallbackStorage exists, use it, else return { allowed: true, remaining: Infinity, resetTime: new Date() } (fail-open). Edge Cases: Invalid key (empty string) throws Error. All errors caught and handled per fail-open strategy.\"},{\"title\":\"Build Express Middleware Factory\",\"description\":\"FILE: src/middleware.ts — function createRateLimiter(config: RateLimitConfig, storage?: StorageAdapter): RequestHandler. Returns async (req, res, next) => { if config.skip?.(req) true, skip. const key = config.keyGenerator(req). const result = await rateLimiter.check(key, config). If allowed: set headers 'X-RateLimit-Limit', 'X-RateLimit-Remaining', 'X-RateLimit-Reset' (UTC seconds), next(). Else: config.onLimitReached?.(req, key). Set Retry-After header = Math.ceil(result.retryAfter/1000). Send 429 with JSON message. }. Edge Cases: Header setting only if not already sent. Timeouts from storage use default Express timeout handling.\"},{\"title\":\"Create Index File and Default Export\",\"description\":\"FILE: src/index.ts — Exports: createRateLimiter (from middleware.ts), RateLimiter class, RedisAdapter, MemoryAdapter, all types. Default export is createRateLimiter. Example usage: import createRateLimiter from './src'; app.use(createRateLimiter({ windowMs: 60000, maxRequests: 10 })); Assumes Redis client passed externally. Edge Cases: Re-export all types for consumer use.\"}],\"keyDecisions\":[\"Redis sorted sets over token bucket because sliding window requires exact time-window counting and sorted sets provide atomic operations with ZREMRANGEBYSCORE.\",\"Lua script over multiple Redis commands because atomicity is critical to avoid race conditions between ZCARD and ZADD in concurrent requests.\",\"Fail-open strategy over fail-closed because rate limiting should not break service availability during Redis outages; log and degrade gracefully.\",\"Storage Adapter pattern over hardcoded Redis to allow pluggable storage (memory, Redis, future DB) and simplify testing with mocks.\",\"Per-request key generator over fixed IP to allow custom identifiers (user ID, API key) via config.keyGenerator, defaulting to IP for flexibility.\",\"Middleware factory over static middleware to enable dependency injection of storage and config, improving testability and reusability.\",\"TypeScript over plain JavaScript for type safety, especially in config and result interfaces, reducing runtime errors.\",\"In-memory fallback with async mutex over lock-free because Node.js single-threaded event loop still needs synchronization for async operations on shared Map.\",\"Retry-After header in seconds over milliseconds because HTTP standard expects seconds, and we compute from resetTime for accuracy.\",\"Cleanup interval in MemoryAdapter over lazy cleanup to prevent memory leak from abandoned keys, with configurable interval defaulting to 5 minutes.\"],\"assumptions\":[\"Express 4.x or 5.x with TypeScript strict mode enabled and @types/express installed.\",\"Redis server 6.x or later available, with node-redis client v4.x installed and configured externally.\",\"Node.js version 16+ with ES2020 support for async/await and private class fields.\",\"Deployment environment supports running Lua scripts on Redis (no restrictions).\"],\"complexity\":\"medium\",\"techStack\":[\"express: ^4.18.0\",\"redis: ^4.6.0\",\"typescript: ^5.0.0\",\"@types/express: ^4.17.0\",\"async-mutex: ^0.4.0\"],\"openQuestions\":[\"Should the middleware automatically add X-RateLimit headers on all responses, or only when limit is approached?\",\"Should there be a global 'enabled' flag to disable rate limiting entirely for certain environments?\",\"How to handle clustered Node.js processes? MemoryAdapter would not share state; should we warn or enforce Redis in multi-process setups?\"]}\n\n╔══════════════════════════════════════════╗\n║  REVIEWER FEEDBACK — MUST BE ADDRESSED  ║\n╚══════════════════════════════════════════╝\nPrevious attempt scored 78/100.\nAttempt 1 of 2.\n\nCRITICAL (must fix):\n  - Compliance Miss (Decision 3): The requirement specified a 'Fail-open strategy... during Redis outages'. While `RateLimiter.check` implements fail-open logic, `middleware.ts` imports and instantiates `MemoryAdapter` directly as a default if no storage is provided (`const storageAdapter = storage || new MemoryAdapter()`). It does NOT implement the fail-open pattern for Redis outages automatically. The user must manually inject a fallback storage into `RateLimiter`. The middleware factory `createRateLimiter` accepts only one `storage` argument. To satisfy Decision 3 fully, the middleware should either accept a fallback adapter or the `RedisAdapter` should handle its own internal fallback, or the factory should facilitate the primary/fallback pattern described in Decision 3 more transparently. Currently, if I pass `new RedisAdapter()`, and it fails, there is NO fallback unless I manually construct `new RateLimiter(redis, memory)` and pass it? No, `createRateLimiter` creates its own `RateLimiter` instance internally, ignoring any external composition. Thus, the fail-open capability is inaccessible via the main middleware export unless the user bypasses the factory.\n  - Security/Correctness in RedisAdapter: The Lua script calculates `resetTime` as `now + windowMs` when allowed. This is technically the end of the current window relative to *now*, but standard sliding windows usually reset relative to the oldest entry in the window or the window boundary. However, the bigger issue is `retryAfter` calculation in `middleware.ts`: `Math.ceil(result.retryAfter / 1000)`. If `retryAfter` is negative (due to clock skew or processing delay), `Math.ceil` might result in 0 or negative headers. Fix: `Math.max(1, Math.ceil(...))`.\n\nMAJOR (should fix):\n  - Critical Bug in MemoryAdapter: `cleanupOldEntries` uses the global `cleanupIntervalMs` (default 5 mins) as the sliding window duration for filtering timestamps (`timestamp > now - this.cleanupIntervalMs`). It ignores the per-request `windowMs` passed to `checkAndIncrement`. This causes valid requests within a short window (e.g., 1 minute) to be incorrectly retained or cleaned up based on the 5-minute interval, leading to inaccurate rate limiting. Fix: The cleanup should ideally not filter by a fixed window, or the adapter needs to track the max window seen, but primarily `checkAndIncrement` handles the windowing. However, keeping stale data for 5 minutes when the window is 1 minute is inefficient. More critically, if the window is > 5 minutes, valid data is deleted prematurely.\n\nMINOR (nice to fix):\n  - Efficiency Issue in MemoryAdapter: `storedData.timestamps.filter` creates a new array on every request. For high-traffic keys, this generates significant garbage collection pressure. Fix: Use a circular buffer or simply shift/push if order matters, or periodically clean up rather than on every hit.\n\nEvery issue listed above must be explicitly fixed in this revision.\n\nOutput JSON:\n{\n  \"code\": \"Complete, compilable source code. Use '// ═══ filename ═══' to separate files. No truncation, no '// ...' placeholders.\",\n  \"explanation\": \"Brief summary of approach and non-obvious choices\",\n  \"files\": [\"Files created or modified\"],\n  \"decisionsOverridden\": [\"Deviations from key decisions with justification — empty array if none\"]\n}"
